Spina Bifida
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Visible midline lumbosacral defect, Lower extremity flaccid paralysis, Neurogenic bladder/bowel, Associated hydrocephalus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-045
Reason for Visit: Acute fever, severe chills, and suspected blocked indwelling urinary catheter causing urosepsis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 32-year-old patient with Spina Bifida and a neurogenic bladder who developed a sudden high fever, uncontrollable shaking (rigors), and suprapubic pain over the past 6 hours, concurrent with a lack of urine output in their leg bag. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the extremely high risk of rapidly progressive urosepsis and septic shock secondary to an obstructed infected urinary tract, requiring immediate catheter change and aggressive antibiotic therapy.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My catheter hasn't drained all afternoon, my lower stomach is killing me, and I am freezing cold and shaking uncontrollably.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 6-hour history of acute systemic illness. The patient has a known diagnosis of Spina Bifida with a visible midline lumbosacral defect (repaired) and lower extremity flaccid paralysis. Due to a neurogenic bladder, the patient relies on a long-term indwelling Foley catheter. Over the past 6 hours, the catheter has ceased draining, and urine is actively bypassing the blocked catheter. The patient has developed severe, colicky suprapubic pain, a high fever, and frank rigors. The patient also suffers from neurogenic bowel dysfunction, reporting severe constipation for the past 4 days, which likely contributed to the catheter occlusion. Associated hydrocephalus with a ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt is noted, but no headache or vomiting is present.
Functional Status: Wheelchair-dependent; relies on partner for complex transfers and bowel/bladder management.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of septic shock; recurrent UTI history.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 39.1 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 70.0 Kg
General: Patient is flushed, visibly shivering (rigors), and in moderate distress due to suprapubic pain.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals a visibly distended, palpable, and exquisitely tender bladder extending to the umbilicus. Bowel sounds are sluggish, and firm stool is palpable in the left lower quadrant. Genitourinary exam reveals an occluded 16Fr Foley catheter with purulent, foul-smelling urine bypassing the meatus. Neurological exam confirms baseline sensory loss below the L2 level and flaccid paraplegia. Cranial nerve exam is normal, and the VP shunt tract is non-tender without overlying erythema. Cardiovascular exam shows tachycardia with border-line hypotension, indicating systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS).
Environmental Observations: A medical alert bracelet prominently notes a severe latex allergy.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine prophylactic antibiotics. Paracetamol taken 2 hours ago with minimal effect on fever.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Spina Bifida (complicated by acute catheter obstruction and impending urosepsis) - Status: Acute, critical complication
Differentials:

VP Shunt Infection/Malfunction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of headache, vomiting, or altered mental status, and the overwhelming, clear localization of the infection to the obstructed, distended bladder.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely as the pain is centrally located over the distended bladder rather than the right lower quadrant, and immediately correlates with the mechanical failure of the catheter.

Nephrolithiasis (Renal Colic) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of high fever, purulent bypass, and a massively distended bladder, indicating lower tract obstruction and infection rather than an isolated upper tract stone.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Impending septic shock)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a high fever, rigors, and tachycardia in the setting of a mechanically obstructed neurogenic bladder. The objective findings of a massively distended, tender bladder and purulent bypass confirmed an acute, severe urinary tract infection trapped behind a blockage. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because an obstructed, infected urinary tract rapidly seeds the bloodstream, leading to fatal septic shock. Immediate, on-site clinical intervention to relieve the obstruction (change the catheter using strictly latex-free equipment) and commence empirical parenteral antibiotics was mandatory to stabilize the patient and prevent hospital admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Removed the occluded catheter. Under strict aseptic technique utilizing 100% silicone (latex-free) equipment, inserted a new 16Fr Foley catheter, instantly draining 900mL of dark, turbid, foul-smelling urine, rapidly relieving the suprapubic pain. Administered a stat intramuscular dose of Ceftriaxone 1g. Prescribed oral Ciprofloxacin to complete a 7-day course.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you drink exactly 2.5 liters of water every single day, you continuously flush the catheter tubing; therefore, carrying a marked water bottle ensures you meet this target and prevents the crystal buildup that causes blockages. When you adhere to a strict bowel regimen using macrogol every second day, you keep your rectum empty; preventing severe constipation removes the physical pressure that squashes and blocks your catheter internally.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to the community continence nurse to review the catheter size and routine change frequency.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensured a supply of 100% silicone catheters is immediately available at home.
Follow-up: GP Telehealth review in 12 hours to verify resolution of the fever and ensure clear, ongoing urine drainage.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department or call 000 if your systolic blood pressure drops below 90 mmHg, if you become confused or dizzy when sitting up, or if the new catheter stops draining and the pain returns.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Systemic infections in patients with neurogenic bladders can be stubborn; the oral antibiotics must be completed entirely even if symptoms resolve quickly.
Clinical Course: The fever and rigors should dramatically improve within 12-24 hours now that the infected urine is draining freely and the antibiotics are in the system.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor your temperature every 4 hours. Seek urgent medical care if you begin vomiting and cannot keep the oral antibiotics down, or if you develop severe lower back pain (suggesting kidney involvement).

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because your bladder nerves don't work, you rely on the tube. The severe constipation pressed against the tube, blocking it, which allowed a severe infection to brew and back up into your bloodstream, causing the severe chills. The new latex-free tube drained the toxic fluid, and the strong antibiotic injection immediately attacks the bacteria in your blood.
Actionable Steps: Drink 2.5 liters of water daily to flush the new tube. Start the oral antibiotics immediately. Treat the constipation aggressively tomorrow. Check your temperature regularly.

